  "text": "Allergies:\nBactrim / Flagyl / Ampicillin\n\nChief Complaint:\n\"No one cares about me anymore, I haven't eaten or drank\nwater in 3 weeks.\"\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\n___ homeless AA F presenting with\ncomplaints of depression and suicidal ideation.  Was BIBA from\n___ of ___.  Ms. ___ states that she is\nprescribed \"neurontin for my mood swings and to help me\nfunction,\" and \"paxil for my depression\" by Dr. ___ at ___. \nShe states that for the past three weeks she has not taken her\nmedication and has instead been giving it to her mother.  \"She\nhas HIV and ___ been giving her my meds because I feel guilty. \nI give her my meds because she has HIV.\"  When I first enter the\nroom the patient is on her cell phone yelling \"Why are you\nhanging up on me?  Fine then, F-you.\"  When I enter she\nimmediately starts crying hysterically and continues for the\nentire interview.  She is difficult to ellicit information from\nas she keeps repeating the same information and does not answer\nquestions well.  She states, through tears, \"No one cares about\nme.  I'm in a relationship with a married man.  He uses me.  He\ntakes my money and my medications and my sex.  But now he says \nhe\ndoesn't want my money.  he doesn't want me.\"  She also states \"I\ndon't want to be homeless.  I don't know how to say no to\nanyone.\" She repeats these phrases a few times, then, ___ been\nin and out of relationships.  I'm with a married man.  I have \nhim\nmy money to be with him but now he told me he didn't want my\nmoney.\"  Ms. ___ states that ___ been staying between her\nmother's home and the married man's house.  She says \"but now \nI'm\ndone.\"  When asked how long ___ been homeless she says \"My\nwhole life.  Because I don't know how to say no to anyone.\"\n\nMs. ___ states \"I don't want to live no more.  Nobody cares\nabout me. There's no hope for me.\"  When asked about if ___\nthought how she'd kill herself she says she hasn't and that, \"I\ndon't want to hurt me.\"  She keeps repeating the above sentences\nover and over again.  When asked about hopelessness she endorses\nthis and then repeats this as well multiple times.  When asked\nabout sleep she says \"No, look at my eyes, I haven't slept in\nthree weeks.\"  This is then repeated as well.  She endorses\ndecreased interests, \"I used to do crossword puzzles.  I don't\neven do that.\"  She endorses decreased energy, appetite, and\nweight loss.  \"I starve myself.  I haven't had water in 3 weeks. \n\nI haven't eaten in three weeks.  ___ given up.  I'm hopeless.  \nI\nhaven't slept in three weeks,\" etc., repeatedly.  She denies HI. \n\nShe is unable to answer any further questions surrounding psych\nROS.  When asked about periods decreased need for sleep and\nincreased energy she continues to repeat \"I haven't slept in 3\nweeks, no energy,\" and is unable to answer questions about\nsymptoms further.    When asked about past psych history she\ndenies any prior hospitalizations.  When asked about diagnoses \nor\nanything further she says \"I have no sleep,\" and continues to\nrepeat statements made above.  \n  The patient endorses daily ETOH, percocets and methadone, \"to\nfunction\" and \"to take the edge off.\"\n\nPer BEST hasn't been seen in a couple years, now on ___ so\nno longer a BEST patient.  Last seen in ___.  Was\ngiven dx of depressive disorder and drug dependence then. Heroin\naddiction.  Her husband had died week before they saw her of\ncancer.  She was using 7 seven bags of heroin per day at the\ntime.  Was homeless at the time.  Was being followed at ___. \nHadn't been sober since ___ at that point.  Had been in\nmethadone treatment as well.  Went to ___ at that point\nfor dual diagnosis ___.  No prior suicide attempts at that\npoint.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\nPSYCHIATRIC\n.\n#) SUBSTANCE INDUCED MOOD DISORDER\nPt dysphoria likely secondary to years of heavy alcohol use. \nInitially, patient commented that she felt much better on the \nunit being sober for several days. Consistently attended groups \nwhich she explained helped her focus on emotional healing and \n\"staying positive.\" However, experienced several episodes of low \nmood and tearfullness secondary to social stressors. Patient \narticulated frustration with lack of sober supports outside \nhospital. Mood lability likely secondary to extended period of \nsobriety, which patient has not had in several years. Explained \nto patient that this was an opportunity to explore/practice new \ncoping mechanisms, whereas in the past she had used alcohol or \ndrugs to escape. While here, restarted paroxetine 20mg qdaily \nand gabapentin 300mg BID. Ultimately increased paroxetine to \n30mg qdaily and increased gabapentin 600mg BID without \ncomplaints of side effects. Patient has appointment with \noutpatient psychiatrist Dr. ___ on ___ at ___ for \nmedication maintenance/follow-up\n.\n#) SUBSTANCE ABUSE\nThe pt was insightful into alcohol dependency (high tolerance, \nextensive time seeking out substances, loss of recreational \nactivities and estrangement from daughter and ___ \nsecondary to alcohol consumption, and drinking more than \nintended amounts).  She was forthcoming about how she has urges \nto drink for decades and can not recall any previous sober \nperiods. Pt still struggling with cravings throughout \nhospitalization and upon discharge. Started patient on 50mg \nnaltrexone daily, since LFTs were within normal limits despite \nbeing Hepatitis C positive (see below). She was amenable to \nattending an ___ rehab program. However, insurance \nwould not cover inpatient stay. Enrolled patient for ___ \nservices, but these services were not yet available upon \ndischarge. Discharged patient to ___ and encouraged \npatient to attend AA/NA meetings near there. Provided a list of \nmeetings. Discussed with patient many sources of motivation for \nsobriety such as reconnecting with her daughter and grandchild.  \n\n.\nGENERAL MEDICAL CONDITIONS\n.\n#) ETOH WITHDRAWAL\nPt started on a CIWA protocol upon admission but did not require \nany valium for withdrawal symptoms.  The CIWA was discontinued \nand at no time did the pt suffer from any \nhallucinations/tremors/seizures. She was given folate and \nthiamin (500 BID) due to her chronic and heavy etoh consumption \nfrom which she has no sober periods in the past. \n.\n#) HYPERTENSION\nPt admitted on 50mg atenolol daily. Blood pressure steadily rose \nduring hospitalization. Highest value was 152/100. Increased \natenolol dose to 100mg daily, and added HCTZ 12.5mg daily. \nPatient denied headaches, vision changes, or any other symptoms \nsuggestive of symptomatic hypertension. Recommended patient \nfollow up with PCP upon discharge for further work up and \nmedication monitoring and administration.\n.\n#) Hepatitis C\nInitially wanted to start patient on naltrexone for alcohol \ncravings, as she is on gabapentin and these two medications work \nsynergistically to decrease cravings. However, hepatitis panel \nsent prior to medication administration came back positive for \nhepatitis C. Did not start naltrexone. Liver function tests \nwithin normal limits. Will encourage her to follow up with PCP \nto monitor values over time.\n.\nPSYCHOSOCIAL\n.\n#) FAMILY CONTACT: Patient explained that major source of mood \nand substance use problems resulted from dysfunctional \nrelationship with mother. Unfortunately, patient lives with and \ncares for mother (who has HIV). Patient realized early on in \nhospitalization that she should not return to live with her \nmother, especially since drugs and alcohol are ubiquitous in her \nmother's home. Patient reported that she has a positive \nrelationship with her aunt, citing her as someone who is sober \nand would support her upon discharge, and someone with whom she \ncould possibly live. However, when patient contacted her in the \nhospital, aunt denied further involvement. Patient extremely \ndiscouraged. However, patient was able to reconnect with her \ndaughter from whom she was estranged for many months. While \npatient cannot live with her daughter, the two have agreed to \nwork on their relationship after discharge. Team was able to \nfacilitate intake appointment with Adcare on ___ at \n9:30. Patient will spend the night of the ___ at a friend's, \n___, with whom patient has been in contact and who \npatient reports is very supportive.\n. \nLEGAL STATUS\nThe pt remained on a CV throughout the duration of her \nhospitalization.\n.\nRISK ASSESSMENT: Patient risk factors include age, depression, \nexcessive alcohol and drug use, single relationship status, and \npoor social support. Protective factors include female gender \nand future intent to maintain sobriety in order to reconnect \nwith her daughter and granddaughter. She also intends to \nestablish herself in the AA/NA commmunity for support.\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nAxis I: Depression NOS\nPolysubstance dependence (opiates, alcohol, r/o amphetamines). \nAxis II: Deferred\nAxis III: Hypertension, HCV\nAxis IV: Financial stressors, homelessness, mother's illness,\npoor social supports\nAxis IV: GAF = 37",
